{% from "partials/icons.njk" import icon %}
{#
  What the desktop app does that nothing else does: it reads a Google Slides
  deck's own speaker notes and keeps up with them as the deck moves.

  This is a description of a feature, not an install page. The extension's two
  halves are offered on /google-slides/ and nowhere else - see the note in
  _data/site.js - because a page that hands a reader an extension to install
  makes it look like a fourth version of CueCard, which it is not. So this
  section says what the sync does and sends anyone who wants it to that page.
#}
<section class="slidesync" id="slides" aria-labelledby="slides-h">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="micro">{{ icon("slides", 13) }}{{ slideSyncMicro or "If you present from Google Slides" }}</p>
        <h2 class="section-h" id="slides-h">{{ (slideSyncHeading or "Your deck already holds the notes. <em>CueCard reads them.</em>") | safe }}</h2>

        <div class="crosspanel reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="crosspanel-tag">{{ icon("layers", 13) }}The browser extension</p>
                <h3 class="crosspanel-h">The notes follow your slides</h3>
                <p class="crosspanel-p">
                    Speaker notes are already written under every slide in your deck. The
                    extension reads them out of Google Slides and hands them to the CueCard
                    window as you move from one slide to the next — so there is nothing to
                    paste before the call, and nothing to scroll for during it. The window
                    stays out of your screen share, the way it always does.
                </p>
                <p class="backline">
                    <a href="/google-slides/">{{ icon("slides", 15) }} CueCard for Google Slides {{ icon("arrow", 14) }}</a>
                    <a href="/google-slides/#extension">{{ icon("layers", 15) }} Install the extension {{ icon("arrow", 14) }}</a>
                </p>
            </div>

            <ol class="slidesteps">
                <li>
                    <span class="slidestep-n">1</span>
                    <span class="slidestep-t">Present from your deck
                        <small class="slidestep-d">In Chrome, Edge, Brave, Arc, Firefox or Safari.</small>
                    </span>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="slidestep-n">2</span>
                    <span class="slidestep-t">Change a slide
                        <small class="slidestep-d">That slide's notes arrive in the CueCard window on their own.</small>
                    </span>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="slidestep-n">3</span>
                    <span class="slidestep-t">Read, and keep sharing
                        <small class="slidestep-d">Your audience sees the deck. The notes are not on the screen they see.</small>
                    </span>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
